INT. COURTHOUSE CONFERENCE ROOM - DAY

A windowless room, laminate table, fluorescent hum. A pitcher of water sweats beside two paper cups. On the wall, a faded print of a ship in a storm.

PHOEBE LINDQVIST, thirties, a public defender with a blunt haircut and a rumpled blazer, arranges folders in neat rows. Her eyes are tired but sharp.

Across from her sits KARIM HADDAD, twenty-eight, in a county-issue orange jumpsuit, wrists cuffed to a ring in the table. He's thin, bearded, with a scar through his left eyebrow and hands that don't stop moving.

Standing by the door, WALT OSTROWSKI, fifties, a bailiff with a broad belly and a kind, tired face, watches the clock.

PHOEBE
The offer expires at noon.

KARIM
What is it?

PHOEBE
Eight years. You serve five.

KARIM
For what I didn't do.

PHOEBE
For what they can prove you did.

KARIM
(quiet)
I was at my mother's.

PHOEBE
Who can say that besides you?

KARIM
She can.

PHOEBE
She's in a coma, Karim.

Karim looks at his hands. Phoebe waits.

WALT
(gently, from the door)
Mr. Haddad, she's right about the clock.

KARIM
I know, Walt.

WALT
Take the deal. You'll be out for your mother's birthday.

KARIM
She won't have one if she's--

He doesn't finish. Phoebe slides a legal pad toward him.

PHOEBE
Write down everything you remember about the night of the ninth. Every detail.

KARIM
I've told it ten times.

PHOEBE
Tell it to the paper.

He takes the pen. Writes. Stops. Writes. Phoebe watches the lines grow, her face unreadable. Walt checks the clock again.

Karim pushes the pad back. Phoebe reads. Her brow lifts at one line.

PHOEBE (CONT'D)
The neighbor's dog.

KARIM
It barked at eleven. I remember because I was changing her IV.

PHOEBE
That's not in the police report.

KARIM
Nobody asked about the dog.

PHOEBE
(rising)
Walt, I need the judge.

WALT
She's at lunch.

PHOEBE
Then I need a sandwich and the judge.

Walt laughs, short, and heads for the door.
